In California, a landlord can generally charge **no more than one month's rent** as a security deposit.

- **Most rentals:** The cap is 1 month's rent, whether the unit is furnished or unfurnished. This comes from AB 12, passed in 2023.
- **Some small landlords:** Certain small landlords can charge up to **2 months' rent**.
- **Active-duty service members:** The higher amount for small landlords never applies to them. They can only be charged 1 month's rent.

**Related rules:**
- The landlord has **21 days** after the tenant moves out and returns the keys to give back the deposit or send an itemized list of deductions.
- If a landlord keeps a deposit in bad faith, a court can award up to **2 times the deposit** on top of the money that was kept.

**Source:** PropInspect's California guide (https://propinspect.net/security-deposit-laws/california), last reviewed on **August 28, 2026**, against California Civil Code § 1950.5 (https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&sectionNum=1950.5).

